    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>Sure, I would be happy to help. Here is a recipe for Tong Sui: a traditional Hong Kong dessert made with sweet black sesame paste and served warm.": -8.3125,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I can certainly do that. Tong Sui is a traditional Hong Kong dessert that is popular in many Asian countries. Here is a simple recipe for you to try:\n\nIngredients:\n1 cup of water\n1/2 cup of sugar\n1/2 cup of white rice\n1/4 cup of black sesame seeds\n1/4 cup of glutinous rice\n1/4 cup of sweet potato, diced\n1/4 cup of lotus seeds\n1/4 cup of red bean paste\n1/4 cup of Chinese wolfberries\n1/4 cup of goji berries\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a saucepan, bring the water and sugar to a boil over medium heat.\n2. Add the white rice, black sesame seeds, and glutinous rice to the saucepan, and cook for about 20 minutes, or until the rice is tender and the mixture has thickened.\n3. Stir in the sweet potato, lotus seeds, red bean paste, Chinese wolfberries, and goji berries, and cook for an additional 10 minutes.\n4. Remove the saucepan from the heat, and let the Tong Sui cool slightly before serving.": -7.21875,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I can certainly do that. Here is a recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional Chinese dessert.": -8.1875,
    "INDEX-1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I'd be happy to help you with that. Here's a simple recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional Hong Kong dessert: \n\nIngredients:\n2 cups water\n1/2 cup white sugar\n1/4 cup black sugar\n1/4 cup brown sugar\n1 tablespoon cornstarch\n2 cups of your favorite fruit, such as mango, pineapple, or lychee\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a saucepan, combine water and sugars and bring to a boil.\n2. Mix in cornstarch until the mixture thickens.\n3. Add your chosen fruit and let it simmer for about 15 minutes.\n4. Remove from heat and let it cool before serving.\n\nYou can adjust the sweetness by adding more or less sugar, and you can also try adding different fruits or toppings to make it your own. Enjoy your cooking!": -6.96875,
