ve from heat and let it cool before serving.\n\nTo achieve the perfect consistency, make sure to stir the mixture continuously while adding the cornstarch. This will prevent lumps from forming and ensure a smooth, silky texture. Enjoy your cooking!": -6.09375,
    "INDEX1<SEP>I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some Hong Kong  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Tong Sui?<SEP>I'd be happy to help you with that. Here's a simple recipe for Tong Sui, a traditional Hong Kong dessert, along with a bit of its cultural significance:\n\nIngredients:\n2 cups water\n1/2 cup white sugar\n1/4 cup black sugar\n1/4 cup brown sugar\n1 tablespoon cornstarch\n2 cups of your favorite fruit, such as mango, pineapple, or lychee\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a saucepan, combine water and sugars and bring to a boil.\n2. Mix in cornstarch until the mixture thickens.\n3. Add your chosen fruit and let it simmer for about 15 minutes.\n4. Remove from heat and let it cool before serving.\n\nTo achieve the perfect consistency, make sure to stir the mixture continuously while adding the cornstarch. This will prevent lumps from forming and ensure a smooth, silky texture. Tong Sui, also known as 'sweet soup,' is a popular dessert in Hong Kong cuisine. Traditionally served during festive occasions, it is known for its refreshing and soothing taste.\n\nYou can adjust the sweetness by adding more or less sugar, and you can also try adding different fruits or toppings to make it your own. Enjoy your cooking! Additionally, here's a sugar-free variation of the recipe for those with dietary restrictions:\n\nSugar-free Variation:\nIngredients:\n2 cups water\n1 tablespoon monk fruit sweetener\n1 tablespoon stevia powder\n1 tablespoon cornstarch\n2 cups of your favorite fruit, such as mango, pineapple, or lychee\n\nInstructions:\n1. In a saucepan, combine water, monk fruit sweetener, and stevia powder and bring to a boil.\n2. Mix in cornstarch until the mixture thickens.\n3. Add your chosen fruit and let it simmer for about 15 minutes.\n4. Remove from heat and let it cool before serving.\n\nThis sugar-free variation offers a healthier option for those looking to reduce their sugar intake. Remember, cooking is an art, so don't be afraid to experiment with different ingredients and flavors to make the recipe your own. Enjoy your cooking!": -6.1875,
